&lt;h2 id=&#34;introduction&#34;&gt;Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This area is known both as Emsworthy and Hemsworthy. Both are considered correct and trace their origins back to the 14th-century Aylmersworthy and 16th-century Omsworthye&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This walk takes us through the Emsworthy area and across to Holwell Tor.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://www.devonwildlifetrust.org/nature-reserves/emsworthy-mire&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Devon Wildlife Trust - Emsworthy Mire&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;routing-box&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;routing-header&#34;&gt;
    &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/icons/signpost.svg&#34;
       alt=&#34;&#34;
       class=&#34;routing-icon&#34;&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;routing-content&#34;&gt;
    &lt;em&gt;When you&amp;rsquo;re ready to leave, walk due West just below the road for 350m, passing the signed footpath to Emsworthy. Notice the levelled ground, this was once a tramway from the Emsworthy mine we approach. Continue to a raised area used as a car park before Emsworthy Gate and cattle grid&lt;/em&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;emsworthy-mine&#34;&gt;Emsworthy Mine
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;This area was originally worked for tin by &amp;lsquo;streaming&amp;rsquo; by the &amp;lsquo;Old Men&amp;rsquo; that could be as far back as the 12th century, reaching its peak in the 1500s. Their work still shows in the steep valleys where they washed raw tin free by directing water streams&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;From the 1780s onwards, small &amp;ldquo;ten fathom&amp;rdquo; shafts were dug to explore this area, and some success was gained, enough to prove the area could be productive. In 1852, the Haytor Consols company started work here and soon sunk an Engine Shaft which eventually reached 168ft. To remove ore, a tramway was constructed which held rails at a 2ft gauge, heading East below the road to take the ore to Crownley Parks for processing. There was also a blacksmith&amp;rsquo;s shop built to service the mine.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite this work, there is no mention of the mine after 1856 suggesting it had been abandoned.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The shaft was later filled or capped and made hardstanding, forming today&amp;rsquo;s car park. At the edges you can still see some large cut sections of granite that may once have formed the engine house foundations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For more details, a sketch map and more photographs, there is an excellent page at Dartmoor Explorations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://dartmoorexplorations.co.uk/hEmsworthy-mine/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Dartmoor Explorations - HEmsworthy mine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
