&lt;h2 id=&#34;emsworthy-farm&#34;&gt;Emsworthy Farm
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The name dates back at least to the 12th century as Aylmersworthy (&amp;ldquo;Aylmer&amp;rsquo;s enclosure&amp;rdquo;) and comprised a typical Devon medieval longhouse with livestock at one end and people at the other. (A fine remaining example of this is Upcott, restored by Dartmoor National Park).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The 1838 Tithe map shows Emsworthy being owned by Lord Cranstoun and farmed by William Winser. It was comprised of 80 acres of arable and rough pasture, a garden, the farmhouse and outbuildings. During Victorian times, the existing stone barn was added as the farm&amp;rsquo;s needs expanded.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Emsworthy Farm was abandoned in the 1870s, although parts remained in use until the First World War. Today, the Barn remains in good order, and foundations and walls show where other buildings and field layouts were, including the walled Emsworthy Lane leading to the farm.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;More details and a map of the area are available at &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://dartmoorexplorations.co.uk/emsworthy-farmstead/&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;Dartmoor Explorations - Emsworthy Farmstead&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Across the valley you may see some strange structures and be curious - these are Show Jumps at Holwell Lawn and regularly used for competition. Surely one of the most picturesque courses in the country.&lt;/p&gt;
