	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
		alt=&#34;Other Side of the gateway&#34;
	
	
		class=&#34;gallery-image&#34; 
		data-flex-grow=&#34;133&#34;
		data-flex-basis=&#34;320px&#34;
	
&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Slotted gateposts aren&amp;rsquo;t unique to Dartmoor, being found throughout the country. Before iron hinges were readily available they were the best way to secure an opening. A single plank or piece of straight timber would be first placed in one of the rectangular slots and then slid horizontally and down in at the other end - the process repeated until all four rails were solidly in place. These examples are so well defined it&amp;rsquo;s easy to see they were designed for either square-cut planks, or squared-off ends. You can also find others at Teignhead Farm and elsewhere - and gateposts still today in use may have modern iron hinge pins either drilled and wedged in place using thin iron &amp;ldquo;tares&amp;rdquo; or slate. More recently - from the 1970s or so, epoxy is used to hold ironwork firm. Other postss may have iron strapping around them to hold the hinge pins, and an iron eye let in on the slamming post.&lt;/p&gt;
